Vitiligo
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Well-demarcated chalk-white macules, Symmetrical periorificial/acral distribution, Absence of scaling/inflammation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-048
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe psychosocial distress and panic attack following the sudden, noticeable spread of depigmented patches on the face.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a young adult experiencing a severe panic attack, hyperventilating, and expressing extreme distress over a "flesh-eating" or disfiguring skin disease rapidly appearing on their face. Urgent assessment was necessary to physically evaluate the lesions, definitively rule out an infectious or malignant process, and provide immediate, authoritative clinical reassurance to de-escalate the acute psychological crisis before morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My skin is suddenly turning completely white around my mouth and eyes. It looks like my pigment is being eaten away, and I'm terrified it's spreading everywhere."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports the insidious onset of small, completely depigmented patches on their hands several months ago, which they largely ignored. Over the past 2 weeks, following a period of intense psychosocial stress, they have noticed a rapid and prominent appearance of well-demarcated chalk-white macules on the face. The distribution is highly symmetrical, with prominent periorificial involvement around the mouth and eyes. The patient also notes a patch of leukotrichia (premature whitening of the hair) within the affected eyebrow. They report a complete absence of scaling, textural changes, active inflammation, pruritus, or pain. The sudden, highly visible facial involvement triggered acute health anxiety.
Functional Status: Independent, currently experiencing a severe acute panic attack.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme psychological distress regarding physical appearance.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 105 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Tearful, hyperventilating, holding a mirror and frequently pointing to the face.
Physical Exam: The face demonstrates several strikingly well-demarcated, completely depigmented chalk-white macules. The lesions are symmetrically distributed, heavily involving the perioral and periorbital regions. Examination of the dorsal hands reveals similar, symmetrical acral depigmentation. Crucially, the skin within the white patches is entirely normal in texture; there is a complete absence of scaling, induration, erythema, or atrophy. A small patch of white hairs is visible in the medial aspect of the left eyebrow. There is no sensory loss within the patches (ruling out leprosy).
Environmental Observations: Clean environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Vitiligo - Status: Active progression, severe psychosocial impact
Differentials:

Tinea Versicolor - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The complete lack of fine branny scaling, the intensely chalk-white color (tinea is typically mildly hypopigmented), and the classic periorificial/acral distribution (tinea prefers the upper trunk).

Pityriasis Alba - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The stark, complete depigmentation and sharply defined borders; pityriasis alba presents as poorly defined, mildly scaly, off-white patches usually on the cheeks of children.

Leprosy (Tuberculoid) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Intact sensation within the depigmented patches, absence of thickened peripheral nerves, and complete lack of an active erythematous border.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to acute severe anxiety and psychosocial impact)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute psychological crisis driven by the sudden, highly visible progression of classic Vitiligo. The striking chalk-white macules in a symmetrical periorificial pattern with an absence of scaling definitively confirm the diagnosis. Triage appropriately flagged the severe panic attack as an urgent presentation. Immediate out-of-hours clinical assessment was absolutely necessary to physically rule out an infectious etiology (which the patient feared) and to provide an immediate, authoritative, and compassionate explanation of the condition. By confirming this is a benign autoimmune process rather than a destructive infection, the intervention successfully aborted the acute panic attack and safely deferred ongoing management to routine care.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Conducted a thorough physical exam to demonstrate normal skin texture. Provided extensive clinical reassurance that the condition is an autoimmune targeting of pigment cells, not an infection, cancer, or "flesh-eating" disease. Coached the patient through deep breathing exercises to resolve the acute hyperventilation.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking slow, deep box-breaths when you feel the panic rising, you will instantly lower your heart rate and regain control of your anxiety, allowing you to process this information calmly. By applying SPF 50+ sunscreen strictly to the white patches every day, you will perfectly protect the fragile skin that no longer has its natural melanin shield against severe sunburns.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Ordered baseline autoimmune screening blood tests (including Thyroid Function Tests) for the GP follow-up.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1-2 weeks to review blood tests, discuss psychological support, and explore specialized dermatological treatments (e.g., topical calcineurin inhibitors or phototherapy).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If you feel completely overwhelmed by anxiety, experience severe chest pain, or develop thoughts of self-harm due to your distress, you must present immediately to the Emergency Department or call a mental health crisis line.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Vitiligo is a highly unpredictable autoimmune condition. The pigment loss may halt spontaneously, or it may continue to progress.
Clinical Course: The skin is physically healthy and structurally sound, but the color change is likely long-lasting. Treatments are available from specialists that can sometimes encourage the pigment to return.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The white patches will burn extremely quickly in the sun. Strict, daily sun protection is mandatory to prevent severe blistering sunburns.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system has mistakenly identified your skin's color-producing cells (melanocytes) as foreign and destroyed them in specific areas. It is not an infection, and it is entirely harmless to your physical health.
Actionable Steps: Stop constantly checking the spots in the mirror, as this drives severe anxiety. Use high-quality sunscreen daily on the affected areas.

